The decision to reject this paper is informed by collective critical concerns from four reviewers, which undermine the paper’s originality, theoretical rigor, experimental validity, and presentation quality. Key concerns include: 
1) Insufficient originality and unacknowledged connections to prior work (e.g., TAC, QNCD), with the core concept of timestep-aware cumulative error correction closely aligning with existing methods but lacking explicit recognition; 

2) Flaws in theoretical foundations, such as overly strong or ambiguous approximations (e.g., Approximation 1 ignoring Jacobian terms, unclear assumptions on quantization error distribution) and critical typographical errors in key derivations; 

3) Deficient experimental design, including mismatched setups for latency evaluation (theoretical derivation based on diffusion solvers vs. latency measured on flow-matching model Flux.1-dev), missing comparisons with error-aware baselines (e.g., QNCD), and incomplete coverage of test conditions; 

4) Unnecessarily complex derivations that obscure core conclusions, and marginal performance gains in image generation tasks that raise doubts about general effectiveness; 5) Reliance on calibration datasets with insufficient analysis of its sensitivity to size and domain in extreme scenarios. These issues collectively fail to meet the publication criteria.
